WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.009
Good afternoon on this warm and lovely day;
welcome, I'm Matthew Spalding, the associate

00:00:31.009 --> 00:00:38.009
vice president Dean here for Hillsdale College
in Washington D.C. Welcome to the Kirby Center.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:44.089
As a college, one that's been around for quite
some time, we have long been interested in

00:00:44.089 --> 00:00:51.089
education. With a graduate school, with undergraduates,
and with our Hillsdale Academy, and increasing

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:58.390
numbers of charter schools we're involved
in. Education is our purpose and reason for

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:03.729
being. As Constitutionalists, we have also
long been interested in the question of the

00:01:03.729 --> 00:01:09.140
government's involvement in education, and
indeed we've also long been concerned with

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.780
and object to the federal government's involvement
in education. If there is one thing that is

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.990
clear from constitutional history and constitutional
study it is that the federal government has

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:25.110
no constitutional authority when it comes
to federal education. That is why as an institution,

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:30.490
from the beginning, we decided we would take
no federal funds, and we would object to any

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:35.490
federal requests having to do with the racial
makeup of our student body, or other information

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:41.340
that we object to. As an institution, we will
continue to object to those things, and looking

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.389
ahead, know that it is probably quite likely
that we will have to fight about some of those

00:01:46.389 --> 00:01:53.389
things. We are increasingly concerned, for
instance, about questions of accreditation.

00:01:54.069 --> 00:01:58.649
The federal government, for some time, has
used the accreditation process, the process

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:05.649
by which universities and colleges are accredited
to teach, as a lever, using student loans

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:12.319
to potentially go after colleges and universities.
That's one of the main reasons why we rejected

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:19.319
those federal funds. With federal funds, strings
are often attached. The student loan program

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:28.590
is one of those strings. That money costs
something. Usually through accreditation,

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:33.120
the federal government will ask for information,
and get colleges and universities to do something

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.720
in order to receive those federal funds. We
don't participate in those programs. But in

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.700
the last administration and the current administration,
the rules of the accreditation process have

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:48.590
changed, such that even though we do not accept
those federal funds, the accreditation process

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:54.980
is increasingly looking to do things to Hillsdale
College through the accreditation process.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:59.920
They are currently looking at it in terms
of credit hours. Our concern is that farther

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.470
down the road, those questions of accreditation
will get into other matters. Whether they

00:03:04.470 --> 00:03:10.440
will have to do with the racial makeup of
our students, which we think is not only constitutional

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:16.420
but unjust, but also questions having to do
with the enforcement of other, social programs

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:20.900
currently of interest to the federal government,
such as the definition of marriage, about

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:27.900
which we object. So, with that, we have a
very important discussion today about curriculum.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.710
If there's one area of all areas of education
that the federal government should not be

00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:39.280
involved in, it's the question of curriculum,
and we are currently involved in this interesting

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.980
debate about something called "the Common
Core." To introduce our speaker who's a very

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:51.980
good, a friend but also a colleague at Hillsdale
College, is one of our students. Morgan Delp

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:58.320
is a junior at Hillsdale College, majoring
in English and minoring in Journalism. She

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:03.900
is captain of the women's tennis team and
sports editor for the Collegian, the college

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.900
newspaper, along with being a member of Kappa
Kappa Gamma. She's originally from Toledo,

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:16.590
Ohio, and she is here in D.C. as part of a
networking in D.C. program the college runs

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:21.780
for students to bring them out to Washington.
Her father is a Hillsdale alumni and her younger

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.780
sister is currently a student there as well.
Morgan.

00:04:31.490 --> 00:04:38.490
My most vivid memory of any one lecture I
have ever attended at Hillsdale College was

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:45.570
in Dr. More's class, when he played the role
of Karl Marx for an entire class period, debating

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:52.570
us on Marxism in a seemingly authentic German
accent. As an assistant professor of history

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:58.470
at Hillsdale College, Dr. Terrance More regularly
makes this kind of effort to inspire his students

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:04.220
in class, and invites them to his home for
dinner to meet his wonderful family as well.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:10.290
A former Marine with a PhD from the University
of Edinburgh, Dr. More served as the founding

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:16.220
principle of a top, K-12 classical school
in Colorado and advises Hillsdale's charter

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.790
school initiative, providing assistance with
the formation of classical charter schools

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:26.970
across the country. Dr. More is the author
of The Perfect Game, and The Story-Killers:

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:32.500
A Common Sense Case Against the Common Core.
This afternoon, he will speak about how the

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:39.500
common core destroys minds and souls. Please
join me in welcoming Dr. Terrance More.

00:05:41.169 --> 00:05:48.169
Thank you very much Dr. Spalding and Miss
Delp, "das is ver goot." And thank all y'all

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:02.460
for coming out, and I hope we have a pretty
rigorous and thought provoking discussion

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:07.889
today about the common core. Now, to realize
what is taking place in the nation's public

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:14.240
schools today, imagine you are an average
teenager in high school. As such, you do not

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:19.010
have the political insight and wisdom you
currently have. In fact, you do not know much

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:23.310
about politics since your family does not
follow political events much. Your parents

00:06:23.310 --> 00:06:28.360
consider themselves moderates and not that
political. Nor do you have any particular

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:32.880
moral convictions other than knowing that
murder is wrong, and a few other thou-shalt-not's,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:38.460
though not too many, because you don't go
to church that often or talk about moral philosophy

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:44.419
or read Aristotle. You think people should
just be nice and get along. Most of what you

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:48.800
know about the world comes from what you learned
from your friends, from the movies you like

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:54.850
and the music, all of recent origin, and from
Facebook. In addition to lacking political

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:59.880
and moral convictions, you're not what a previous
era would have called educated. Sure, you

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:04.840
make good grades and you will go to college,
yet you have never studies closely any great

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.120
work of literature or political event. Nor
do you know a foreign language, though you've

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:16.120
taken Spanish for years. Nor are you even
that well versed in the grammar of your own

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:22.600
language. Certainly you have read things in
school but nothing has really ever stuck in

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.060
large part because you have never been made
to study anything thoroughly. In short, you

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:33.110
are a typical high school student in America.
But all of a sudden, out of the clear blue

00:07:33.110 --> 00:07:38.500
sky, comes a set of rigorous standards, as
the people in charge of your schools call

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:45.500
them, that are going to make you college and
career ready, for a 21st century global economy.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:51.280
That is the promise. That is the rhetoric;
but what of the reality? Will these so called

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:55.560
standards in the curriculum and teaching that
go with them really make you college and career

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:01.680
ready? In greater concern, what will these
standards do for your mind and soul? Let's

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.520
find out. Among the other things you will
learn in your English classes of all places

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.750
is that the Constitution was written by a
group of men who did not really believe that

00:08:10.750 --> 00:08:16.050
the words "we the people" referred to women,
or black men, or men who did not hold property,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:21.400
or anyone in short who were not white, land-owning
men like themselves. For that reason, the

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:27.639
Constitution must be "evolving," which, as
you learn from your science class, means always

00:08:27.639 --> 00:08:33.310
changing to meet the needs of the present
time. You also learn, in a lesson from the

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:38.430
unit on the Civil War, that racism is still
almost as bad today in our times as it was

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:44.560
in sojourner truth's time. And in fact, your
teacher gives you explicit instructions on

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:48.940
what to do if you are the witness of a racial
incident: instructions that come straight

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:54.680
out of her teacher's edition to the textbook,
that say, "Individuals experiencing or witnessing

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:58.860
discrimination should record the name of the
person doing the discriminating and report

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:04.899
the incident as soon as possible. Witnesses
should also do their best to take care of

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:09.060
the victims if there is any physical injury.
They should also make it clear that they saw

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:15.080
what happened and that it will be reported."
This is in an English class. More political

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:21.100
lessons come in the English class. You read
a New York Times editorial from 1999, which

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.690
states emphatically that Mikhail Gorbachev
almost single-handedly brought down the Wall,

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:31.550
and ended the Cold War. You do read a speech
of Reagan in the class, but not his first

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:36.140
inaugural, or his speech at the Brandenburg
Gate (that's the one in which Reagan said,

00:09:36.140 --> 00:09:40.850
"Mr. Gorbachev, tear down this wall."); in
fact, you don't even know that it's on YouTube,

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:45.050
and could be shown to the class, but the teacher
doesn't bother to do that, because it's not

00:09:45.050 --> 00:09:50.920
in the standards. Instead, you read a speech,
a little-known one, given to the students

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:56.320
of Moscow State University, in which the editors
of your literature textbook preface it with

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:02.110
this statement: "Led by Mikhail Gorbachev,
the Soviets were blazing through the greatest

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:08.270
changes they had seen since the 1917 Revolution.
Although reforms were rapidly taking route,

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:13.930
they were not far enough from Communist ideology
for Reagan. In this excerpt [that is, the

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:19.040
speech that follows], notice how Reagan restrains
his strongly anti-Communist sentiments, while

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:25.620
still extolling the ideals he represents."
Now that's the Reagan you remember, right?

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.290
Or rather, your grandparents remember, because
remember, you're all high school students.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:35.930
The Reagan who was always restraining his
strongly anti-Communist sentiments. Well,

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:40.399
that's how it happened according to the public
schools, and the new college and career ready

00:10:40.399 --> 00:10:44.709
standards. So the lesson seems to be that
Reagan, at his rhetorical best, had to pull

00:10:44.709 --> 00:10:48.670
back on his strongly anti-Communist sentiments,
thereby not getting in the way of Mikhail

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:55.060
Gorbachev and the Russian people blazing through
historical changes. You also learn from your

00:10:55.060 --> 00:11:01.620
English classes how and why to fill out government
forms. Your teachers are prompted by their

00:11:01.620 --> 00:11:06.899
manuals to ask these questions: What kinds
of information are typically sought be government

00:11:06.899 --> 00:11:12.620
forms? Why do you think governments require
this information? And you're given the example

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:16.610
of a driver's license in order to show you
that filling out government forms is usually

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:22.810
for your own protection and safety. Thus making
you all, I guess, willing bureaucrats. Because

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:27.640
every teenager wants to drive, right? Another
lesson will be a couple of selections on the

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:34.040
theme of World War II; well, not World War
II so much as Hiroshima, and Hiroshima told

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.770
from the view of certain Japanese people who
were still in the city the morning the bomb

00:11:37.770 --> 00:11:44.279
was dropped. No other perspective on World
War II will be offered. No speeches of Churchill,

00:11:44.279 --> 00:11:49.310
or from Roosevelt, no reports of the liberation
of Europe, not even a word about how evil

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:53.860
the Nazis were. And certainly not the fact
that hundreds of thousands of warning pamphlets

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:59.420
were dropped on Hiroshima in an effort to
get people out of the city. All you will know

00:11:59.420 --> 00:12:05.730
is about the horrible killing that Americans
did on that forlorn group of people. But your

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:10.380
English class will not just stick to the writings
of the past. Quite likely you will get to

00:12:10.380 --> 00:12:16.330
read recently written informational texts,
taken out of contemporary periodicals, as

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:21.610
we shall see in a moment. Would you ever get
to read any, well, literature, in your high

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:28.610
school literature class? Well, yes, sort of.
While the brave new standards which your teachers

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:35.339
mention frequently do not recommend the reading
of any Mark Twain in the high school, and

00:12:35.339 --> 00:12:40.459
you never even see a copy of Huckleberry Finn,
your textbook does contain four different

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:45.890
illustrations of Twain, that is, pictures
of him, a brief selection from his Life on

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:52.890
the Mississippi, and the six page The Notorious
Jumping Frog of Calaveras County, basically

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:58.360
the shortest story that Twain ever wrote.
So you spend about a day and a half on Mark

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:05.240
Twain. The treatment of that great American
author pales in comparison to the reading

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:10.370
of The Crucible, which takes a hundred and
seven pages, in the course of which you will

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:14.930
also learn about the evils of McCartheyism,
how there never really were any communists

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:20.620
in Hollywood, and how right-wingers in general
are not to be trusted. After your complete

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:24.480
reading of The Crucible, you would be given
the treat of reading a selection from that

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:31.480
great author, George Clooney. Specifically,
part of his screen play of Good Night and

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:38.830
Good Luck, about Edward R. Murrow, who was
taking on McCarthey. Now let's pause a minute;

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:43.360
if you are a person who likes to put things
in the perspective of numbers, then you will

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:49.970
want to know what the present score is. It's
Mark Twain's Jumping Frog, six pages; Arthur

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:56.970
Miller's The Crucible, 107 pages; John Hersi's
Hiroshima, eleven pages (with lots of illustrations

00:13:58.029 --> 00:14:04.209
of the damage done by the bomb); and George
Clooney, six pages. That sounds about right,

00:14:04.209 --> 00:14:09.410
doesn't it? That's not the only literature
you will encounter. You will get to read two

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:16.410
whole chapters out of the 135 chapters found
in Herman Melville's Moby Dick. Unfortunately

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:21.040
one of those chapters is the last, so if you
ever decide to read the whole book, the ending

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:27.579
has already been spoiled for you. I believe
on Netflix they call that a spoiler alert.

00:14:27.579 --> 00:14:32.790
In accordance with the special standards you
hear so much, you're supposed to read Jane

00:14:32.790 --> 00:14:37.670
Austin's Pride and Prejudice. In your textbook,
however, that great novel is only suggested

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:43.680
for independent reading. No selection of an
Austin novel appears, but you do get to read

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:48.910
a letter from Austin to her neice, a letter
which is interpreted by the helpful editors

00:14:48.910 --> 00:14:55.910
of your textbook as making her into a proto-feminist,
specifically an unconscious critic of her

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:01.760
society. Now that's what Jane Austin was,
right? An unconscious critic of the society

00:15:01.760 --> 00:15:08.760
she so much loathed? Wow. You will get to
read a bonified feminist in the tale The Story

00:15:09.459 --> 00:15:15.760
of an Hour, by Kate Chaupin. In this story,
a woman, named Mrs. Mallard, is told that

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:22.350
her husband died in an accident. After a moment
of grief, and just a moment, she is overcome

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:28.970
by a sense of relief, and then a freedom.
She stretches out her arms to welcome the

00:15:28.970 --> 00:15:35.500
future, which will be "hers alone." To aid
your understanding of this story, the omnipresent

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:40.360
editors of the textbook invite you to interpret
the action of Mrs. Mallord as a symbol of

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:47.360
"the negative aspects of 19th century marriage."
Now wasn't 19th century marriage sort of like

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:57.670
marriage? Recall what our scenario was at
the beginning. If you were a teenager in high

00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:02.529
school and went through this way of learning
literature, would you be college and career

00:16:02.529 --> 00:16:07.200
ready? Well I'm a college professor, and I
teach freshmen every year from all over the

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:12.450
country, and I can answer that question with
an emphatic no! The students going off to

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:17.470
college these days, at least those who come
from the public schools, for the most part

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:24.470
cannot have a serious discussion about even
one work of literature. Not one. The freshman

00:16:24.870 --> 00:16:28.490
year of college -- and there are some freshmen
in here, or at least some sophomores, and

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:32.660
they can tell you whether I'm lying or not
-- the freshman year of college everywhere

00:16:32.660 --> 00:16:37.910
in the country is a crash course in learning
how to read and write anything beyond a basic

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:43.290
level. Learning, in short, what high school
did not teach you but should have taught you.

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:48.529
And the new standards that we're talking about
are not going to help that; in fact, they

00:16:48.529 --> 00:16:55.529
will make it worse. A more important question,
it seems to me, is this: what kind of mind,

00:16:55.630 --> 00:17:02.200
indeed, what kind of soul would you have,
after going through this sort of stuff? Well

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:06.209
you would probably think that your country,
the good old US of A, had been up to nothing

00:17:06.209 --> 00:17:12.130
but mischief in its almost 240 year history,
to say nothing of the awful Puritans who settled

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:17.319
this land. You would think of the past as
living under one giant black cloud, and thus

00:17:17.319 --> 00:17:21.470
be under the impression that the past has
nothing to teach us. You would have little

00:17:21.470 --> 00:17:27.299
or no appreciation for beauty, or heroism,
or faith, because you would never have discussed

00:17:27.299 --> 00:17:32.840
such things in relation to a story. You would
not have too have of an opinion of the family

00:17:32.840 --> 00:17:38.880
as an institution, or even the love that holds
families together, because no such models

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:44.429
would have ever been provided for you. Nor
would you have been invited to love the thing

00:17:44.429 --> 00:17:49.900
we call the good. Almost as bad, you would
not have been taught how to laugh, and how

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:54.179
to find humor in the human condition, another
lesson conveyed by the great authors, such

00:17:54.179 --> 00:17:59.820
as Austin and Twain that is utterly lost on
the modern makers of state standards and the

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:05.230
publishers of monstrous textbooks. In short,
your English classes would have taken you

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:11.280
down one of two roads: that of utter boredom,
caused by all the nonsense you had to suffer,

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:16.720
or, if you actually took these lessons seriously,
down the depressing path of a prematurely

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:23.720
jaded, postmodern, antiheroic view of life.
In the latter case, you would have been intellectually

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:29.520
and morally debilitated. In either case, you
would not have learned from literature the

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:36.480
most important thing: how to be more human.
Stories, you see, are what shape our view

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:43.480
of the world. As Plato told us long ago, whoever
controls the poets and the storytellers controls

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:50.740
the regime. Whoever controls what today we
call the narrative, controls the politics,

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:57.740
the economics, the family, the ways of thinking,
and the ways of believing. The most impressionable

00:18:59.299 --> 00:19:05.890
people, of course, are always the children.
So welcome to the world of the common core.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:11.870
The common core, at least as far as the English
standards are concerned, and as later will

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:15.850
be the case with the History standards, which
are right around the corner, is the attempt

00:19:15.850 --> 00:19:20.710
to take away the great stories of the American
people and replace them with the stories that

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:27.000
fit a progressive liberal narrative of the
world. As such, the architects of the common

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.570
core are nothing less than story-killers;
and that's the title of my book, in which

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:38.669
I lay this case out in greater detail. They're
story-killers. They are deliberately killing

00:19:38.669 --> 00:19:44.710
the greatest stories of the greatest nation
in history. Now if they are doing this, how

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:50.530
are they managing to pull this off, right
under our very noses, you may wonder. Furthermore,

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:55.510
why would any self-professed school reformers
and alleged conservatives have endorsed the

00:19:55.510 --> 00:20:01.260
common core? These are really good standards,
we are told. And besides, even if some of

00:20:01.260 --> 00:20:05.360
the textbooks get things wrong, we should
keep these standards, and allow the wizards

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:09.960
at our state departments of education and
in our local school district bureaucracies

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:15.340
to come up with some really rigorous curricula
that fit the standards. So, as we have been

00:20:15.340 --> 00:20:20.740
told by the nation's secretary of education,
we should stop listening to these Tea-Partiers,

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:27.740
and above all, these suburban moms. I have
two responses to those questions, and we can

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:33.130
discuss them at greater length if we need
to. First, the discussion over education in

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:38.070
this country has been impoverished for so
long that anything proposed as a school reform

00:20:38.070 --> 00:20:44.710
can look attractive to people who are grasping
for any solution, any remedy. In a town that

00:20:44.710 --> 00:20:51.110
doesn't have a doctor, sometimes the snake
oil salesman looks pretty good. Second, the

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:55.740
architects of school decline are not just
going to stand up and announce that they intend

00:20:55.740 --> 00:21:00.250
to take away your children's chances of learning
the great literature, and thus becoming more

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:06.900
humane as well attack the foundational principles
of this country. Progressives in the old days

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:13.110
were remarkably forthright about their intentions.
Today, sadly, that is not the case. So what

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:18.820
would we expect to find are the real intentions
of such designs obscured in both veiled language

00:21:18.820 --> 00:21:25.390
and empty promises. To expect otherwise would
mean being the most naïve kid on the block:

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:29.539
the kid who thinks that the neighborhood bully
will like you if you just give him enough

00:21:29.539 --> 00:21:34.240
candy. So let's do a bit of code breaking
and figure out what the authors of the common

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:40.510
core state standards are really up to. To
begin with, the purposes of education advocated

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:47.510
by the common core are insufficient, illiberal,
and covertly ideological. All we hear about

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:54.650
from the common core is college and career
readiness for a 21st century global economy.

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:59.390
You may be used to this rhetoric surrounding
education, and wonder what's wrong with it.

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:05.000
Several things: first, the authors do not
show any study, or quote any college president,

00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:09.669
calling out for this kind of education. The
authors of the common core are simply asserting

00:22:09.669 --> 00:22:14.700
that their scheme will make students college
and career ready, with no proof to back them

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:21.520
up. The plan has not been tested in any school
in the country. Now that is astonishing. As

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:26.750
Miss Delp told you, I ran a successful school
for seven years. With Hillsdale, I help set

00:22:26.750 --> 00:22:32.270
up schools all over the country. I can point
to actual schools where incredible things

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:36.480
are taking place in the classroom. I can tell
you the teachers, and the classes, and the

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:41.900
books that you ought to go see. The common
core can't do that. It can point to no such

00:22:41.900 --> 00:22:48.080
successes. They cannot give you the address
of a single school of theirs, except the addresses

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:51.970
of all the failing schools in the nation that
have been following the progressive blueprint

00:22:51.970 --> 00:22:58.970
for a long time. So in other words, the 45
states that have adopted the common core standards

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:05.970
with little, almost no, public discussion,
bought the farm sight unseen. Second, let's

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:11.510
think about the ends of education as being
college and career readiness. When was this

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:16.690
phrase invented? When did you first hear it?
I never really heard it until a couple of

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:22.140
years ago. But now it is all the rage. Now
you may say without reflecting that college

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:27.610
and career readiness are aims of a K-12 education.
We want our children to go to college, don't

00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:33.780
we? We want them to get good jobs. But those
are byproducts of education, not the essential

00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:40.780
aims. What are the essential and traditional
aims of education? Truth, beauty, goodness,

00:23:44.539 --> 00:23:51.539
virtue. What about inculcating virtues, such
as courage, justice, industriousness, and

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:57.220
prudence? For almost 400 years in this country
and for over 2,000 in the history of the West,

00:23:57.220 --> 00:24:02.679
truth and knowledge and beauty and virtue
were the aims of education. Our founding fathers,

00:24:02.679 --> 00:24:06.299
in particular Benjamin Franklin and Thomas
Jefferson, both wrote on education, and they

00:24:06.299 --> 00:24:13.190
both said the same thing about the fundamental
aim of education. Do you know what it is?

00:24:13.190 --> 00:24:20.190
Happiness. The end at which all other things
result in. Where is that in the common core?

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:28.130
There is no happiness in the common core.
Now you may think that this is a remote professorial

00:24:28.130 --> 00:24:33.110
point, but it is fundamental and has practical
significance. If an important aim of education

00:24:33.110 --> 00:24:39.960
is beauty, then you will have a robust program
in music in art. If your aims are college

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:44.919
and career readiness, where does art fit in?
The dirty little secret is that art and music

00:24:44.919 --> 00:24:50.620
are dying a slow death in our schools. Furthermore
literature, the discipline next to art and

00:24:50.620 --> 00:24:57.429
music that speaks most directly to the human
soul, is seen as increasingly dispensable.

00:24:57.429 --> 00:25:02.559
But let's ask ourselves a simple question.
Do we in the 21st century require less beauty

00:25:02.559 --> 00:25:09.200
than they did in the 18th, or the 19th? Have
we got beauty down pat? Is the new app called

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:16.150
self-control enough to give our young people
self-control? The authors of the common core

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:19.770
want us to believe that everything must change
in order for education to keep up with the

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:25.580
times. They use alluring statements such as
21st century literacy. Now think about that.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:30.390
This is the phrase that's on the second page
of the common core state standards, 21st century

00:25:30.390 --> 00:25:37.390
literacy. When Y2K came, did you automatically
become illiterate, because you had learned

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:42.780
to read and write in the 21st century? I think
what's more likely is that the children of

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:48.340
the 21st century who grew up on texts and
tweets and three letter codes such as OMG

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:55.340
and TMI are struggling to acquire that long-lost
20th century literacy, to say nothing of how

00:25:55.840 --> 00:26:01.080
people spoke and wrote during the age of the
nation's founding. Moreover, one of the major

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:07.990
components of the common core is to put children
behind computers for hours of the day, as

00:26:07.990 --> 00:26:13.120
though they do not already spend enough time
behind computers when they're not at school.

00:26:13.120 --> 00:26:17.210
By trying to educate young people mostly for
jobs and increasingly through the use of the

00:26:17.210 --> 00:26:24.210
computer, we fail to understand two aspects
of the human mind. First, jobs do not make

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:31.730
the human mind; the human mind makes jobs.
Second, children are human beings, they are

00:26:36.169 --> 00:26:43.169
not machines. They can be taught; they cannot
be, or at least they should not be, programmed

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:50.669
like machines. Under the cover of college
and career readiness and 20th century literacy,

00:26:50.669 --> 00:26:55.860
the common core diminishes substantially the
amount of literature read in English class.

00:26:55.860 --> 00:27:00.520
The authors of the common core are actually
up front about this. On page 5 of the standards,

00:27:00.520 --> 00:27:04.780
they say that students in elementary school
should read 50% of what they call informational

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:11.730
texts and 50% literary texts. That number
goes up to 70-30 to the disadvantage of literature

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:18.190
by the time they reach high school. So what
ends up going away? The great stories. What

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:23.110
does this mean in practice? It means that
students will no longer be reading, or reading

00:27:23.110 --> 00:27:27.770
complete, works of literature, especially
since the format of standardized testing results

00:27:27.770 --> 00:27:33.130
and drive-by's of the literary curriculum,
rather than the close, patient following of

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:38.220
a complete literary work. Furthermore, it
means all of a sudden that obscure writings

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:45.220
of modern authors, many of them simply journalists,
will have the same status as Shakespeare.

00:27:46.029 --> 00:27:50.049
Now how does the common core pull this off?
Why would we throw away great literature and

00:27:50.049 --> 00:27:55.850
replace it with common articles from periodicals
or worse. I'll give you an example of one

00:27:55.850 --> 00:28:02.850
of these periodicals. Atol Gwandes, the Cost
Connundrum: Health Care Costs in McAllen,

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:10.580
Texas, published in the New Yorker in 2009.
That's supposed to be in the students' English

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:17.580
curriculum. Now, why are we talking about
health care in the summer of 2009? Does anybody

00:28:19.860 --> 00:28:25.570
remember? So how do they pull this off? Well,
it begins with these things called standards.

00:28:25.570 --> 00:28:30.900
Don't be fooled by the word "standards." Although
this is a word that we love to throw about

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:34.380
in education circles, these standards are
essentially meaningless. Let me give you an

00:28:34.380 --> 00:28:41.380
example. Here's a standard, RL2-9 -- standards
for literature, second grade, standard 9 -- reads

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:47.650
as follows: "By the end of the year, read
and comprehend literature, including stories

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:54.650
and poetry, in the grades 2 through 3 complexity
band proficiently, with scaffolding as needed

00:28:55.480 --> 00:29:02.480
at the high end of the range." Sounds impressive,
right? Well if you actually translate that

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:07.820
standard, especially if you get to the word
"scaffolding," which essentially just means,

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:12.330
it's the metaphor of the scaffold, that which
you have to build up a building and then it

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:18.429
goes away, it could be retranslated as help,
or teaching. If you translate that standard,

00:29:18.429 --> 00:29:21.650
which I will read again: "By the end of the
year, read and comprehend literature, including

00:29:21.650 --> 00:29:27.950
stories and poetry" -- of course, what else
would literature consist in? -- "in the grades

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:34.470
2 through 3 complexity band proficiently with
scaffolding needed at the high end of the

00:29:34.470 --> 00:29:39.909
range." Or, "Students in second grade should
read and understand more difficult books at

00:29:39.909 --> 00:29:46.909
the end of the year than at the beginning.
They may need help though." Now I wish all

00:29:49.850 --> 00:29:53.130
of the people who've lauded these standards
all over the country and talk about how rigorous

00:29:53.130 --> 00:29:58.850
they are, I would like them to explain where
the rigor is in that piece of obvious drivel

00:29:58.850 --> 00:30:04.450
and semi-common sense. This bit of absurdity
is actually worse when we follow it to its

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:09.940
conclusion. I have looked at these recommended
second through third grade books. They have

00:30:09.940 --> 00:30:15.070
mostly been written in the last ten to twenty
years, there are no classics, no fairy tales,

00:30:15.070 --> 00:30:19.890
no tall tales, no works by famous authors.
Many of the books are written at a level far

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:24.640
below that of a third grader. One of the books,
called Tops and Bottoms, is actually listed

00:30:24.640 --> 00:30:29.679
on the Amazon page as being intended for four
through eight year olds. That's not the top

00:30:29.679 --> 00:30:35.600
end of the range. Another, called Cowgirl
Cate and Cocoa, is a monosyllabic story about

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:40.250
a girl who wants to herd cows with her horse,
but the horse keeps telling her that she is

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:45.289
thirsty or hungry, and there are a lot of
pictures to go along with this story. Cute

00:30:45.289 --> 00:30:50.980
for a four year old, perhaps, but nothing
as exciting and multi-layered as Aladdin and

00:30:50.980 --> 00:30:56.320
the Magic Lamp, which does not appear in the
common core documents. Therefore this standard

00:30:56.320 --> 00:31:01.669
that seems formidable by the way it is written
is just either common sense, or its an empty

00:31:01.669 --> 00:31:08.669
promise. You see, the emperor really has no
clothes; but I can't use that allusion, because

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:16.340
no Hans Christian Anderson appears in the
common core. None! No Little Mermaid, no Little

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:22.220
Match Girl, no Ugly Duckling, no Emperor Has
No Clothes., or no Common Core with No Clothes

00:31:22.220 --> 00:31:28.659
-- which would be an awful sight, if you think
about it -- and therefore no lasting memories

00:31:28.659 --> 00:31:35.659
of great stories in childhood, thus attenuating
the child's soul. How does the common core

00:31:37.669 --> 00:31:41.890
justify the erosion of great literature, and
its replacement with simplistic stories, and

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:46.340
forgettable journal articles? That's where
Appendix A of the standards comes in. And

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:51.390
I'm telling you this to save you having to
read Appendix A, which is a mind numbing journey

00:31:51.390 --> 00:31:58.390
into obscurity and psychobabble and educational
nonsense. Appendix A does not simply say "read

00:31:59.549 --> 00:32:04.429
the classics." That would be too easy, and
that's not what they're about. Instead, what

00:32:04.429 --> 00:32:09.029
they do is come up with this idea of text
complexity, and they make the astonishing

00:32:09.029 --> 00:32:15.029
statement that students, as they grow older,
should read more and more complex texts -- as

00:32:15.029 --> 00:32:20.620
though that was ever questioned. But to make
this text complexity effective, they create

00:32:20.620 --> 00:32:24.470
this thing, or they have this thing, called
the lexial framework, which measures a text

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:28.870
complexity based on a computer mathematical
model, basically. So you churn in a text,

00:32:28.870 --> 00:32:35.539
and out comes a text complexity rating. Now,
that's stupid to begin with, because when

00:32:35.539 --> 00:32:39.500
I look at an algebra problem, I don't ask
of the algebra problem, "You know, this doesn't

00:32:39.500 --> 00:32:44.980
quite have the sense of humor that Mark Twain
has?" So why should we try to apply a mathematical

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:51.980
model to a story? Even so, the lexial framework
is woefully inadequate. It ranks, get this,

00:32:53.870 --> 00:32:59.929
Steinbeck's The Grapes of Wrath -- all 581
pages of it -- at a second to third grade

00:32:59.929 --> 00:33:06.929
level. Hand your young child or grandchild,
who is eight -- and I have a son who's eight

00:33:08.320 --> 00:33:15.039
-- a copy of The Grapes of Wrath, and see
what he does with it. Meanwhile, forgettable

00:33:15.039 --> 00:33:19.130
journal articles like the one we talked about
in the New Yorker, they get bumped up to the

00:33:19.130 --> 00:33:24.080
high school level, because they're exceedingly
complex. Now let's think about this. If you

00:33:24.080 --> 00:33:29.500
were to churn Appendix A of the common core
state standards into the lexial framework

00:33:29.500 --> 00:33:35.289
because of all its jargon, it would get bumped
up probably to the college level. Meanwhile,

00:33:35.289 --> 00:33:40.620
Huckleberry Finn, which is not recommended
by the common core, would probably be what,

00:33:40.620 --> 00:33:47.270
at a fourth grade level? It's artificially
elevating these so called informational texts

00:33:47.270 --> 00:33:52.460
above great works of literature, and it's
doing it for a reason. Appendix A is mainly

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:55.960
spent on this complexity business, but they
do have another category in there that they

00:33:55.960 --> 00:34:01.279
like, and that's called range. They say that
there must be a range of authors. What do

00:34:01.279 --> 00:34:07.299
they mean by a range of authors? Well for
the most part they mean post-modern authors,

00:34:07.299 --> 00:34:13.529
multicultural, in other words, other-that-traditional
authors, because they want a lot of those

00:34:13.529 --> 00:34:19.639
put into the curriculum every year of the
class, every year that literature's taught.

00:34:19.639 --> 00:34:24.510
So if you were trying to spend an entire year
on the classics in the freshman year, and

00:34:24.510 --> 00:34:27.829
then another year on British literature in
the sophomore year or something like that,

00:34:27.829 --> 00:34:32.049
you would be violating one of the fundamental
principles of the common core, which says

00:34:32.049 --> 00:34:37.299
you always have to have a range of authors.
And guess what? Standardized tests are going

00:34:37.299 --> 00:34:44.190
to require that you have a range of authors.
At any rate, let's get to the red meat of

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:49.169
the actual classroom lessons. And before we
do that, which I want to do through the textbooks,

00:34:49.169 --> 00:34:54.099
I want to say something about Appendix B.
A lot of people have really spend some time

00:34:54.099 --> 00:34:58.640
on Appendix B, because that's this long list
of exemplar texts that you're given. And I've

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:02.920
had state legislators for example say, "Well
there are good texts listed in here. Shakespeare

00:35:02.920 --> 00:35:07.799
is listed," so on and so forth. First of all,
Appendix B is window dressing. That's the

00:35:07.799 --> 00:35:12.739
first thing. The second thing is Appendix
B basically sets up these drive-by's of literature

00:35:12.739 --> 00:35:16.739
that I was just talking about because they
cram in too many authors. There is an overwhelming

00:35:16.739 --> 00:35:23.519
number of Post-Modern authors in Appendix
B. But then on top of that Appendix B is lacking

00:35:23.519 --> 00:35:29.829
in certain things, which as soon as you think
of them, then make common sense. So for example,

00:35:29.829 --> 00:35:36.829
there are no texts inspired by religion that
I can find in Appendix B. Obviously you don't

00:35:38.099 --> 00:35:43.049
find the Bible anywhere written. Why would
you have the opening chapters of Genesis,

00:35:43.049 --> 00:35:48.759
which is probably the most formidable and
formational story in all of Western Literature.

00:35:48.759 --> 00:35:54.509
Of course you wouldn't have that, because
it's public school, right? And then, on top

00:35:54.509 --> 00:35:59.660
of that, they don't have anything inspired
by the Bible. So there's no Saint Augustine,

00:35:59.660 --> 00:36:05.799
though they like autobiography. There's no
Milton, there's no Sinners in the Hands of

00:36:05.799 --> 00:36:10.430
an Angry God, which is a sermon that used
to be read in every American literature class

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:15.359
in the country. There's nothing inspired by
the religious tradition. No why does this

00:36:15.359 --> 00:36:20.890
matter? Well, we used to speak of our culture
in terms of being descended from two parents:

00:36:20.890 --> 00:36:25.469
Athens on the one hand, Jerusalem on the other.
What's going on in the common core standards

00:36:25.469 --> 00:36:29.819
is they've killed off one of the parents,
that's Jerusalem, and the other one is ailing.

00:36:29.819 --> 00:36:35.999
So pretty soon we'll be cultural orphans.
On top of that, here's another egregious example

00:36:35.999 --> 00:36:42.249
that I find. There's no Benjamin Franklin
recommended anywhere in the standards. Now

00:36:42.249 --> 00:36:46.890
in the old days, almost everybody in high
school, I know this from looking through my

00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:51.130
grandfather's books, almost everybody read
Benjamin Franklin's autobiography -- at least

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:56.969
a big chunk of it. It's not even recommended
by these wizards who are behind the work group

00:36:56.969 --> 00:37:03.969
that put these authors forward. None of his
satires, such as Edict from the King of Prussia,

00:37:04.849 --> 00:37:08.789
even though the common core standards claims
to like satire, and says that it needs to

00:37:08.789 --> 00:37:15.789
be taught. Why is there no Benjamin Franklin?
Is it that they didn't think of it? That's

00:37:16.999 --> 00:37:22.430
possible. Or it could be that they don't want
Franklin in the common core standards because

00:37:22.430 --> 00:37:29.430
what it Franklin? He's the anti-bureaucrat.
He's the great American. He's the man of self

00:37:29.469 --> 00:37:35.420
help. He has a scientific mind, and a rigorously
scientific mind. You would think that this

00:37:35.420 --> 00:37:39.769
would be the example of somebody who could
explain college and career readiness. He invented,

00:37:39.769 --> 00:37:43.709
he created a college, called the University
of Pennsylvania. Why wouldn't we have him

00:37:43.709 --> 00:37:48.680
as a model of this new kind of person who's
going to go out and who's going to make things

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:53.069
and create things? Because that's not the
model that they're actually after. So there's

00:37:53.069 --> 00:37:57.019
no Benjamin Franklin in the standards and
there's no apologies about it. But there are

00:37:57.019 --> 00:38:03.519
plenty of Post-Modern authors that do make
it into the standards, so that should raise

00:38:03.519 --> 00:38:08.519
question marks. Well, let me give you a couple
of examples of some of the things that I'm

00:38:08.519 --> 00:38:13.259
talking about. Because when you actually get
into the textbooks, you descend into the world

00:38:13.259 --> 00:38:20.029
of absurdity and bias and lost opportunities.
Now I'll give you two examples. One comes

00:38:20.029 --> 00:38:25.739
straight out of the common core standards
themselves. You can find this in Appendix

00:38:25.739 --> 00:38:32.029
B. In the sixth through the eighth grade range,
a portion of the Constitution is recommended

00:38:32.029 --> 00:38:36.700
for reading. That portion specifically is
the preamble to the Constitution and the first

00:38:36.700 --> 00:38:43.130
amendment. Not the second amendment, not the
tenth amendment -- too much for middle school

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:48.089
students. But you're just told, okay, so read
part of the Constitution. What they don't

00:38:48.089 --> 00:38:54.369
tell you, is that also in Appendix B on page
95, is they have a reading to go along with

00:38:54.369 --> 00:38:58.869
the reading of the preamble and the first
amendment. This is straight out of a modern

00:38:58.869 --> 00:39:05.109
commentary by Linda Monk called Your Annotated
Guide to the Constitution, which goes like

00:39:05.109 --> 00:39:12.109
this: "The first three word of the Constitution,"
that's what it says, "The first three word

00:39:13.619 --> 00:39:18.759
of the Constitution are the most important.
They clearly state that the people, not the

00:39:18.759 --> 00:39:23.630
king, not the legislature, not the courts,
are the true rulers in American government.

00:39:23.630 --> 00:39:28.650
This principle is known as popular sovereignty."
Okay, so far so good, except that I would

00:39:28.650 --> 00:39:34.009
point out that in 1787 there was no longer
a king in America to be referred to, but we'll

00:39:34.009 --> 00:39:39.259
leave that for another time. "But who are
'we the people'? This question troubled the

00:39:39.259 --> 00:39:44.890
nation for centuries. As Lucy Stone, one of
America's first advocates for women's rights,

00:39:44.890 --> 00:39:51.459
asked in 1853, ''We the people'? Which 'we
the people'?' The women were not included,

00:39:51.459 --> 00:39:55.969
neither were white males who did not own property,
American Indians or African Americans slave

00:39:55.969 --> 00:40:02.319
or free. Justice Thirgood Marshall, the first
African American on the Supreme Court, described

00:40:02.319 --> 00:40:09.319
the limitation:" here's Marshall: "'For a
sense of the evolving nature of the Constitution,'"

00:40:09.509 --> 00:40:16.209
can we turn up the sound? "'For a sense of
the evolving nature of the Constitution, we

00:40:16.209 --> 00:40:21.109
need look no further than the first three
words of the document's preamble, 'we the

00:40:21.109 --> 00:40:26.380
people.' When the founding fathers used this
phrase in 1787, they did not have in mind

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:31.739
the majority of American citizens. The men
who gathered in Philadelphia in 1787 could

00:40:31.739 --> 00:40:36.880
not have imagined, nor would they have accepted
that the document they were constructing would

00:40:36.880 --> 00:40:41.709
one day be construed by a Supreme Court to
which had been appointed a woman and a descendant

00:40:41.709 --> 00:40:48.709
of an African slave.'" Now we don't have time
to go into all the errors and inaccuracies

00:40:48.719 --> 00:40:53.509
and half-truths of that statement. If you
want to, then read my colleague Tom Wes' great

00:40:53.509 --> 00:40:57.369
book Vindicating the Founders. For starters
we could start out by saying that Article

00:40:57.369 --> 00:41:03.910
1 Section 2 of the Constitution turns over
the franchise to the states to decide who

00:41:03.910 --> 00:41:09.359
could vote. At the time of the ratification
of the Constitution there were actually women

00:41:09.359 --> 00:41:16.359
and black men voting in this country. That
is not pointed out. But the point is, "we'll

00:41:18.029 --> 00:41:21.130
let the students read a little bit of the
Constitution," if you open up Appendix B and

00:41:21.130 --> 00:41:24.119
you're a state legislator you think "oh, they're
going to read a little bit of the Constitution,

00:41:24.119 --> 00:41:29.359
that's great." What's actually happening is,
this outside expert is going to be brought

00:41:29.359 --> 00:41:34.769
in in one of the students' textbooks, and
the founders are going to be trashed as racists,

00:41:34.769 --> 00:41:40.650
as chauvinists, as extremists, as hypocrites.
That's what happens. And I'm not making this

00:41:40.650 --> 00:41:46.309
up, this is straight out of the common core
state standards. Let me give you another example.

00:41:46.309 --> 00:41:52.809
This one's a little bit funnier. Let's say
that you were told that your students were

00:41:52.809 --> 00:41:57.479
going to spend a week or so on Frankenstein.
Mary Shelley's Frankenstein. What would you

00:41:57.479 --> 00:42:04.479
think that would entail? The reading of Frankenstein,
right? Wrong! You're not operating according

00:42:05.459 --> 00:42:12.130
to the logic of the great arch testers. Instead
of Frankenstein, Mary Shelley's actual words,

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:17.039
what you would have are three and a half pages
of Mary Shelley's actual words -- not from

00:42:17.039 --> 00:42:23.190
Frankenstein the novel, but from her writing
about what it was to write about Frankenstein.

00:42:23.190 --> 00:42:28.489
You would have 5 pages of a modern author
telling about scary stories that she had when

00:42:28.489 --> 00:42:34.430
she was growing up, and how scary they were,
in no reference what so ever with anything

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:39.619
having to do with Frankenstein. You would
also have a scary picture, or really a hokey

00:42:39.619 --> 00:42:44.509
picture, of Frankenstein himself out of a
1950's movie, and there would be a discussion

00:42:44.509 --> 00:42:50.089
in the class about why the monster looked
so scary in this 1950's movie. You would be

00:42:50.089 --> 00:42:57.089
given assignments such as "Write an autobiography
of a monster." Alright, now think about that.

00:42:57.619 --> 00:43:01.509
You're writing an autobiography of a monster,
you're being asked to become a monster and

00:43:01.509 --> 00:43:08.479
write your own story. Why? Well you're told
it's because monster stories are always told

00:43:08.479 --> 00:43:14.309
from the perspective of the humans, and we
need to gain greater insights into monsters,

00:43:14.309 --> 00:43:21.269
and how they must feel in being called monsters.
I am not making this up, this is true. Even

00:43:21.269 --> 00:43:28.269
better, five and a half pages, more than the
actual words given to Mary Shelley, are given

00:43:28.559 --> 00:43:35.559
to a script, live from New York. It's Saturday
Night Live. A Saturday Night Live skit on

00:43:38.729 --> 00:43:44.979
Frankenstein, from which I will read you a
portion.

00:43:44.979 --> 00:43:48.569
Villager 1 to Head Villager: Maybe you're
the monster!

00:43:48.569 --> 00:43:53.630
Head Villager shakes his head: I'm not the
monster. (points to Frankenstein's monster)

00:43:53.630 --> 00:43:58.739
Look at him! He's got a square head and green
skin!

00:43:58.739 --> 00:44:05.009
Frankenstein's Monster: Oh great. Now it's
a racial thing. You know what? You guys are

00:44:05.009 --> 00:44:09.440
a bunch of fascists.

00:44:09.440 --> 00:44:12.650
In case the students don't understand what's
going on here, the teacher, in the margins

00:44:12.650 --> 00:44:19.140
of the teacher's edition, is asked to explain
the term fascist, point out the use of the

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:26.140
term fascist, explain its traditional political
meaning, and how it has been extended to refer

00:44:26.440 --> 00:44:33.440
to any right-wing extremist group. Does anybody
in here belong to a right-wing extremist group?

00:44:37.079 --> 00:44:44.079
Because you may be a fascist! So, the scariest
thing about the common core, though some of

00:44:49.949 --> 00:44:54.369
the things I've said are scary, or in some
cases just ridiculous, the scariest thing

00:44:54.369 --> 00:44:58.999
I actually think is written on the first page
of the introduction to the common core English

00:44:58.999 --> 00:45:04.339
standards. And I will read that. Right after
we've been told that we need college and career

00:45:04.339 --> 00:45:10.699
readiness in a 21th century globally competitive
society, this statement appears: "The standards

00:45:10.699 --> 00:45:17.079
are intended to be a living work. As new and
better evidence emerges, the standards will

00:45:17.079 --> 00:45:23.719
be revised accordingly." Isn't that sort of
like the living constitution we currently

00:45:23.719 --> 00:45:28.640
have, which is revised according to whatever
Supreme Court Justice wants to construe it

00:45:28.640 --> 00:45:35.170
in light of socioeconomic factors? Are we
to have a living curriculum to go hand in

00:45:35.170 --> 00:45:41.009
hand with our living Constitution? Who's going
to get to decide what constitutes better evidence?

00:45:41.009 --> 00:45:44.390
And what is the new evidence that's going
to emerge? Do you think any of the new evidence

00:45:44.390 --> 00:45:48.729
will tell us that studying Latin actually
improves vocabulary and studying grammar actually

00:45:48.729 --> 00:45:54.019
helps with understanding the English language?
That new evidence is not going to be there.

00:45:54.019 --> 00:45:58.749
The standards are a living work, which means
they will be rewritten and rewritten again,

00:45:58.749 --> 00:46:05.239
and that even anything that might be good
in them currently will be a forgotten memory

00:46:05.239 --> 00:46:10.819
ten years from now. What state legislators
have signed on to, they have no control over

00:46:10.819 --> 00:46:17.819
whatsoever. Well this is I think the way that
the modern progressives are controlling the

00:46:19.499 --> 00:46:26.499
narrative, the story telling, in our nation's
classrooms. And they pulled it off -- at least

00:46:26.630 --> 00:46:31.729
they had 45 states adopt it really quickly
with almost no public discussion. But I think

00:46:31.729 --> 00:46:36.789
they overshot themselves, and they made two
fundamental mistakes. One is, they didn't

00:46:36.789 --> 00:46:42.459
think that the American people would want
to fight for its stories. They thought that

00:46:42.459 --> 00:46:47.160
the American people, with the promises of
a globally competitive society -- as though

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:52.949
we've never seen that before -- somehow would
embrace computers and new technology or every

00:46:52.949 --> 00:46:58.479
newfangled idea in education, and forget the
fact that we as a nation understand what it

00:46:58.479 --> 00:47:02.539
means to be a globally competitive society,
and what we should be doing in the classroom

00:47:02.539 --> 00:47:08.479
is forming the minds and souls of the nations
youth. And therefore we need our stories,

00:47:08.479 --> 00:47:14.199
because the stories are the things that form
and educate the heart. The second thing that

00:47:14.199 --> 00:47:19.880
they overshot and did not expect is that they
did not, or they simply underestimated, the

00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:26.880
suburban mom. There is nothing that a suburban
mom, or any mom for that matter, cares more

00:47:27.609 --> 00:47:34.609
about than the heart and the happiness of
her children. And when that comes into danger,

00:47:35.599 --> 00:47:42.289
suburban moms, who vote and who know how to
organize themselves, as two ladies in Indiana

00:47:42.289 --> 00:47:47.930
do, named Heather Crossen and Erin Tuttle,
and who can form societies like Hoosiers Against

00:47:47.930 --> 00:47:54.930
the Common Core, they will mobilize people
and they will take action. And state legislators

00:47:55.229 --> 00:48:00.670
then have to listen. So what we're doing right
now, what is going on in this country and

00:48:00.670 --> 00:48:06.309
I don't know how much Washington D.C. understands
this, but the issue that's boiling right now,

00:48:06.309 --> 00:48:12.439
other than Obama Care, in the country itself,
in the states, is common core. And this is

00:48:12.439 --> 00:48:17.449
a fight over our schools, and ultimately the
souls and the minds of our nation's young

00:48:17.449 --> 00:48:24.029
people. And so I would say to you, and I would
say to the folks who are listening to us today,

00:48:24.029 --> 00:48:28.969
that this is the time to take our stories
back, and that after we do that we can take

00:48:28.969 --> 00:48:34.539
our schools back, and once we have our schools
back, we're on the road to taking our nation

00:48:34.539 --> 00:48:41.539
back. Thank you.

00:48:58.459 --> 00:49:05.459
We have time for a few questions, I know your
time is brief here. If you would identify

00:49:05.650 --> 00:49:12.519
yourself and keep your question brief and
to the point so we use our time wisely.

00:49:12.519 --> 00:49:19.519
Hi, I'm Deb Ray from Maryland. One of my biggest
concerns is, I understand about the standards,

00:49:24.069 --> 00:49:31.069
and our school district is all about the standards.
And then when we question the Maryland Board

00:49:32.689 --> 00:49:39.670
of Education about the curriculum, they say
the curriculum is written by the local schools.

00:49:39.670 --> 00:49:44.430
So could you maybe touch on that a little,
because I guess I'm a little confused about

00:49:44.430 --> 00:49:44.599
that.

00:49:44.599 --> 00:49:47.969
Well it's deliberately confusing I think.
So first of all you have these things that

00:49:47.969 --> 00:49:52.729
are talked about all the time called the standards.
And the standards are just educational jargon,

00:49:52.729 --> 00:49:58.329
as I showed, and I do this in more detail
in my book. I would suggest going in and looking

00:49:58.329 --> 00:50:02.130
how these standards can be translated. And
so what a lot of these state legislators and

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:06.939
local districts will say is, "Well the standards
we'll keep, but we get to decide at the local

00:50:06.939 --> 00:50:10.880
level what the curriculum is." Number one,
when has a school district ever stood up and

00:50:10.880 --> 00:50:13.660
said "we're going to get rid of these awful
textbooks and we're actually going to have

00:50:13.660 --> 00:50:17.920
students read complete novels." Has any district
in the country said that? I haven't heard

00:50:17.920 --> 00:50:22.410
it. That's the first thing. The second thing
is, who controls the curriculum nowadays?

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:28.349
Well, whoever controls the testing controls
the curriculum. Because every teacher knows,

00:50:28.349 --> 00:50:32.939
and the districts will swear this is not the
case, but the teachers know that they're going

00:50:32.939 --> 00:50:38.180
to teach to the tests, and they panic until
they're given a class in a box in the form

00:50:38.180 --> 00:50:43.880
of a teacher's manual and all this other cutchermol
that now goes with the textbooks, and they're

00:50:43.880 --> 00:50:48.949
going to teach to that. And the textbooks
publishers are writing exactly what's going

00:50:48.949 --> 00:50:52.789
to be on these tests. So whoever controls
the testing controls the curriculum, period.

00:50:52.789 --> 00:50:57.869
That is the reality of public schools these
days. And since the tests are flawed, the

00:50:57.869 --> 00:51:01.579
curriculum is going to be flawed.

00:51:01.579 --> 00:51:08.369
So then the curriculum is really coming down
with the standards.

00:51:08.369 --> 00:51:08.989
Absolutely.

00:51:08.989 --> 00:51:14.949
We just found out that they're going to be
eliminating MSA's, this is their last year,

00:51:14.949 --> 00:51:19.299
Maryland State Assessments. Do you know what's
going to happen next year?

00:51:19.299 --> 00:51:21.259
You mean in Maryland?

00:51:21.259 --> 00:51:21.749
Yeah.

00:51:21.749 --> 00:51:25.289
Well, what's happening in most the states,
and I don't know the specifics of Maryland,

00:51:25.289 --> 00:51:29.299
is that there are one of these two testing
organizations, one's called Park and the other's

00:51:29.299 --> 00:51:35.140
called Smarter Balance, and those are going
to be the test makers. And certain states

00:51:35.140 --> 00:51:39.839
are saying, "Well, we just won't use Park,"
or, "We won't use Smarter Balance," but the

00:51:39.839 --> 00:51:44.170
folks who are behind this common core, they're
not dummies, and they can create another shadow

00:51:44.170 --> 00:51:47.579
organization to come right back in and give
a similar sort of test that's going to be

00:51:47.579 --> 00:51:52.670
exactly what you would get out of a Park or
Smarter Balance test. So as long as you have

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:56.880
adopted as a state these standards, there's
going to be some testing that supposedly mirrors

00:51:56.880 --> 00:52:02.819
these standards, which is just the way, it's
the hammer to enforce the kind of stuff that's

00:52:02.819 --> 00:52:06.089
going on that I just was talking about.

00:52:06.089 --> 00:52:13.089
Hello, Scott Walter from Capital Research
Center. You've done a great job of explaining

00:52:13.180 --> 00:52:17.269
that this type of education form is not at
all desirable, but what I'm curious about

00:52:17.269 --> 00:52:23.789
is how do you think public schools can in
fact be reformed with any of these problems?

00:52:23.789 --> 00:52:30.789
Okay that's a great question. The best remedy
right now for the public schools is school

00:52:33.849 --> 00:52:39.709
choice, because it breaks up the monopoly
and gives the chance of independent people,

00:52:39.709 --> 00:52:45.069
sometimes parents, to come together and make
a school that's good. Now not every charter

00:52:45.069 --> 00:52:48.150
school or every school of choice ends up being
great, because a lot of them are just purely

00:52:48.150 --> 00:52:53.369
progressive, some of them are worse than regular
district schools. But it gives the opportunity

00:52:53.369 --> 00:52:59.029
to form more traditional, I would say classical
school that then come in there and do a great

00:52:59.029 --> 00:53:04.029
job and show what you can actually do with
public money. Now why this is important at

00:53:04.029 --> 00:53:08.559
this moment is, at the very moment where we're
seeing really great things happening with

00:53:08.559 --> 00:53:12.619
school choice, particularly with the classical
school movement, and I could point you to

00:53:12.619 --> 00:53:17.160
the states and the schools, Colorado, Arizona,
this is going on. At the very moment when

00:53:17.160 --> 00:53:23.140
that's happening, in comes the common core
and universalizes, nationalizes his uniform

00:53:23.140 --> 00:53:30.140
curriculum which will have the effect of stamping
out school choice. See what I mean? Because

00:53:30.499 --> 00:53:35.839
everybody's got to conform to the test. Even
more frightening, it's making inroads into

00:53:35.839 --> 00:53:41.369
private schools. Most of the moms around the
country who are upset had their kids in private,

00:53:41.369 --> 00:53:47.880
parochial, or Catholic schools, and it's taking
over those curriculum as well. Homeschoolers

00:53:47.880 --> 00:53:51.559
are worried because there's a lot of talk,
and this is probably going to happen, that

00:53:51.559 --> 00:53:56.999
the major testing industries for getting into
college, the college entrance exams, are going

00:53:56.999 --> 00:54:02.999
to be aligned with the common core. So everyone
is affected; nobody is out of this. But going

00:54:02.999 --> 00:54:07.779
back to your regular question, let's have
school choice, which allows parents to see

00:54:07.779 --> 00:54:12.549
what kind of great schools can be set up,
and then it's a matter of parents taking action

00:54:12.549 --> 00:54:18.579
to get enough of these kind of schools. And
either the districts reform themselves or

00:54:18.579 --> 00:54:25.579
they go the way of the dinosaurs -- which
wouldn't be a bad thing.

00:54:34.219 --> 00:54:40.670
Name is Nelson Keef. Isn't the problem the
mind of that freshman that you spoke about

00:54:40.670 --> 00:54:47.670
early in your lecture, and the ability of
that mind to critically analyze and think

00:54:48.930 --> 00:54:54.849
about what questions to ask in the pursuit
of truth, beauty, happiness, and virtue? Isn't

00:54:54.849 --> 00:55:01.769
it therefore an issue of how do you open and
teach that mind to think for itself rather

00:55:01.769 --> 00:55:07.329
than substitute one stream of narrative for
another stream of narrative?

00:55:07.329 --> 00:55:11.699
I think I understand your question, but let
me give you an example of what you're talking

00:55:11.699 --> 00:55:17.400
about. Will students have the capacity to
think, and a lot of thinking occurs through

00:55:17.400 --> 00:55:24.099
asking questions. Two things are not to be
found in the common core standards, one in

00:55:24.099 --> 00:55:30.380
math, the other in literature. In the math
that's taught in common core, in the geometry

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:36.619
classes, proofs are not taught. Euclidian
proofs. Everyone says what? It's the obvious

00:55:36.619 --> 00:55:41.479
answer! What is geometry without proofs? Well
it's not geometry. It's not the geometry that

00:55:41.479 --> 00:55:46.410
we've had for 2000 years, it's not the geometry
that Abraham Lincoln learned in a log cabin,

00:55:46.410 --> 00:55:51.929
which taught him how to think through from
first premises. That's gone. You know what

00:55:51.929 --> 00:55:58.929
else is not in the common core? Plato. There's
no model of a Socratic discussion. Now if

00:56:00.039 --> 00:56:04.869
you open up the standards, you'll have, "Oh,
and students should raise their hands in discussion

00:56:04.869 --> 00:56:09.109
and be able to compliment their neighbor on
the ridiculous thing that he said" and so

00:56:09.109 --> 00:56:12.969
on and so forth. There's all that kind of
stuff in the common core. These standards

00:56:12.969 --> 00:56:16.509
are this long, and they're actually supposed
to be written on the board, a lot of principles

00:56:16.509 --> 00:56:22.259
are making their teachers write them on the
board, so the students know how boring life

00:56:22.259 --> 00:56:29.259
has become. All of that verbiage could be
replaced with one word: Socratic. But to make

00:56:31.019 --> 00:56:35.089
that word viable, to make it mean something,
you'd have to have a Platonic dialogue, such

00:56:35.089 --> 00:56:39.679
as the Apology. That's not in the common core,
and they don't want it in the common core.

00:56:39.679 --> 00:56:46.679
Because I don't think they want those kind
of minds that you're talking about.

00:56:53.259 --> 00:56:57.809
Nat Nasworth with the Christian Post. So if
I understand you correctly, you're saying

00:56:57.809 --> 00:57:03.929
that the common core is being used as a vehicle
to bring liberal ideas and philosophies to

00:57:03.929 --> 00:57:10.439
students, and I wonder if you think that's
an intentional thing, or if it's simply the

00:57:10.439 --> 00:57:15.890
people who came up with the suggestions, suggested
readings and so forth, just pick things that

00:57:15.890 --> 00:57:19.640
they liked, and so it kind of turned out that
way.

00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:24.239
That's an interesting question, more of a
psychological one, because it's a chicken

00:57:24.239 --> 00:57:28.289
and egg argument, right? Did the people who
made the common core standards, do they think

00:57:28.289 --> 00:57:34.499
like this? Yes. So it may not be that they
consciously thought, "Okay well let's just

00:57:34.499 --> 00:57:38.670
give them the liberal progressive narrative."
That's just the way that they think. But I

00:57:38.670 --> 00:57:45.609
read the results, and when I look at the results,
they're crystal clear. The things that I've

00:57:45.609 --> 00:57:51.679
found in these textbooks and in the standards
themselves are just appalling, they're obvious,

00:57:51.679 --> 00:57:56.380
they're blatant. And it takes a real art to
write such a confusing document that most

00:57:56.380 --> 00:58:00.999
people can't interpret it, I give them that.
But that's just to say that they're extremely

00:58:00.999 --> 00:58:07.999
bad writers. So why would we put them in charge
of the nation's writing?

00:58:12.289 --> 00:58:17.630
My name's Steve Allan, I'm a farmer from Iowa,
and I have grandkids back there. And you mentioned

00:58:17.630 --> 00:58:22.650
during your speech you're working with schools
around the country. Do you have a list of

00:58:22.650 --> 00:58:27.219
schools that you're working with say in Iowa
or elsewhere that we might channel our kids

00:58:27.219 --> 00:58:28.579
in that direction?

00:58:28.579 --> 00:58:35.579
As far as Iowa, I don't know of any classical
school movement going on, I could be wrong,

00:58:37.619 --> 00:58:42.670
maybe private schools there are, among the
charter schools I don't know of any. One of

00:58:42.670 --> 00:58:48.179
the funny things about Iowa is, you've had
the Iowa Test of Basic Skills for so long,

00:58:48.179 --> 00:58:52.099
that's what I took when I was a kid, which
are not curriculum tests, they're basically

00:58:52.099 --> 00:58:58.029
logical thinking tests with language and math,
which are perfectly inoffensive, by the way.

00:58:58.029 --> 00:59:03.299
That's the great thing about them. They don't
tell you whether students have been taught

00:59:03.299 --> 00:59:07.329
the knowledge they should have gained, but
at least they measure people's understanding

00:59:07.329 --> 00:59:14.170
of language and math without an objective,
without an agenda. But Iowa has been I think

00:59:14.170 --> 00:59:17.809
a little bit on the complacent side, and many
states in the Midwest are this way, in thinking

00:59:17.809 --> 00:59:23.029
"well, the public schools aren't as bad here
as they are everywhere else, and besides that

00:59:23.029 --> 00:59:27.019
we have this Iowa Test of Basic Skills which
other states use, so we must be doing something

00:59:27.019 --> 00:59:34.019
right." So I would actually like to see a
more aggressive classical or charter school

00:59:34.089 --> 00:59:40.029
movement taking place in Iowa. But, it may
be and I just haven't gotten wind of it. I

00:59:40.029 --> 00:59:45.150
will say that education, and this is what
Dr. Spalding started out by saying, education

00:59:45.150 --> 00:59:50.609
is and ought to be a local issue. And so this
is one that everybody should be involved in

00:59:50.609 --> 00:59:55.199
because you're citizens and you pay taxes
into your local school district. And so if

00:59:55.199 --> 01:00:01.579
your taxes are going towards creating a kind
of citizen and even a kind of human being

01:00:01.579 --> 01:00:06.650
who's very unlike what the Founding Fathers
had thought this nation ought to be, and very

01:00:06.650 --> 01:00:13.650
unlike, frankly, what nature says we ought
to be, then you have a dog in that fight,

01:00:14.199 --> 01:00:18.839
and you need to fight that issue as hard as
you can. And that I think is what is happening

01:00:18.839 --> 01:00:22.859
in the common core. It's galvanizing a lot
of people who weren't in the fight, and that's

01:00:22.859 --> 01:00:23.529
a great thing.

